**Proposition 7.3 (two exact obstructions to insufficient reductions).** Ordinary eight-function Chebyshev structure and separate angular/failure marginals do not justify a universal two-saturated or seven-TOTAL restriction for this problem.

Proof. On eight distinct pure-sheet points the last three full moment functions vanish, so their eight-point evaluation determinant is identically zero. The ordinary Chebyshev premise fails. For marginal insufficiency take four equally weighted points $(u,v)=(\pm\sqrt\alpha,\pm\sqrt\beta)$. Their capacity is one common constant $c_0$ because it depends only on the squares. Saturating the two points with $uv>0$, versus those with $uv<0$, leaves the angular law, sheet mass, $\eta\operatorname{cap}$ and $\eta\operatorname{cap}u^2$ identical, but $\sum p\eta\operatorname{cap}uv$ is respectively $+c_0\sqrt{\alpha\beta}/2$ and its negative. This is an exact failure of the proposed marginal representation; neither law is asserted to meet the required zero physical cross-covariance. Theorem 7.2 supplies a physical post-event counterexample to the universal two-saturated/seven-TOTAL optimum restriction. $\square$

